> identify y' - 3*y = exp(t)
identify y' - 3*y = exp(t):
  an ordinary differential equation of order 1 in y(t)
  linear: every y-term appears to the first power [proved: the partial derivatives in y, y', ... do not involve y]
  inhomogeneous: forcing term exp(t)
  constant coefficients
  characteristic equation: r - 3 = 0, roots 3
  homogeneous solutions: y(t) = a combination of exp(3*t)
  not autonomous: it depends on the independent variable explicitly
  first order: y' = exp(t) + 3y
  separable: no [numeric, 40 point pairs: g(t1, y1) g(t2, y2) = g(t1, y2) g(t2, y1)]
  exact (M + N y' = 0 with M = -3y - exp(t), N = 1): no [numeric: dM/dy - dN/dt = -3]
  solved for the highest derivative: y' = exp(t) + 3y  (then initial values and: dsolve y)
> identify y''' + y = 0
